1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.060
The brief said: redesign the UX of five learning platforms.

2
00:00:05.061 --> 00:00:08.209
The research said the brief was wrong.

3
00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:13.602
And it ends with a customer telling me my improvement made things worse.

4
00:00:14.686 --> 00:00:23.196
Customers didn't recognise any of our five product names — they already called all of it PTC University.

5
00:00:23.197 --> 00:00:28.751
Underneath, the perpetual-license business model was the broken interface.

6
00:00:28.752 --> 00:00:30.885
Not the navigation.

7
00:00:30.886 --> 00:00:35.683
So I asked PTC to kill four of its five products.

8
00:00:35.684 --> 00:00:41.176
Five platforms became one, under the name customers already used.

9
00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:46.271
A million dollars a year off the print budget.

10
00:00:46.272 --> 00:00:52.039
Subscriptions from zero to sixty-four percent of new bookings in a year.

11
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.990
A hundred and fifty thousand active learners migrated in twenty-four months.

12
00:00:57.991 --> 00:01:07.027
Product and design lead, twenty-fourteen to twenty-nineteen — first design hire, then the product's owner in practice.

13
00:01:08.158 --> 00:01:10.945
The part that didn't work.

14
00:01:10.946 --> 00:01:16.223
Toyota used engineering images beside assessment questions.

15
00:01:16.224 --> 00:01:26.418
Reasoning that engineers needed detail, I replaced the inline image with a small thumbnail that enlarged on click.

16
00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:32.918
Toyota came back: enlarging and closing it broke the pace of the assessment.

17
00:01:32.919 --> 00:01:38.751
I had optimised for inspection, and taxed the actual task.

18
00:01:38.752 --> 00:01:45.987
The fix put the large image back, with full-screen zoom kept as an option on top.

19
00:01:45.988 --> 00:01:51.584
When adoption stalls, check the contract under the screens first.

20
00:01:51.585 --> 00:01:55.571
The full case — the miss included — is below.
